hypotheses.statement:
The walks of a group can be rearranged, without changing the multiset of their graph edges, into one-turn layers of period $p$. Each layer has an integer function $z:\mathbb Z\to\mathbb Z$ such that $$\label{eq:layer-properties}
z(x-1)\le z(x),\qquad z(x-1)\ge x,
\qquad z(x+p)=z(x)+p.$$ At start coordinate $x$, its windows are exactly $$\label{eq:layer-vertical}
[x,e),\qquad z(x-1)\le e\le z(x);$$ it then takes a down step to start $x+1$. The functions of the layers in a group are pointwise ordered. Each layer has up cost $p$, and the sum of these costs over all groups is $W$.

Consider $k$ ordered layers with a common period-$p$ text $A$, and put $K=kp$. Let $R=[a,b)$ spell a word in $V$ agreeing with $A$. Let $r_H=f_H(a)$ be the top layer’s first end and $r_L=l_L(a)$ the bottom layer’s last end. If $$\label{eq:collective-conditions}
r_H\le b+K,\qquad r_H-r_L\le K,
\qquad r_L+K\ge b,$$ there is a cost-$K$ closed walk based at the word of $R$ that touches all $k$ layers.

Let a host layer have period $q$. On that layer choose actual windows $R_i=[a_i,b_i)$, indexed by a nonempty collection of distinct child groups. The text of child group $i$ has period $p_i$, is different up to translation from the host’s text and from every other child group’s text, and agrees with the host throughout $R_i$ in the chosen alignment.*


*For each $i$, one or more child layers request a connection at this same record. Each has a specified actual window $[a_i,e)$ satisfying $$\label{eq:request-condition}
a_i+p_i<e\le b_i-p_i.$$ Its period-$p_i$ budget is reserved for the request. If the host’s period-$q$ budget is also available, the host and all requesting layers can be rooted using at most these budgets.

This procedure stops with $1\le k\le n$. If $L_*$ is the lowest of the top $k$ layers, then $$\begin{aligned}
l_{L_*}(t)&\ge H-(k-1)p,\label{eq:sharp-band}\\
f_H(x)-l_{L_*}(x)&\le K\qquad(x\in\mathbb Z).\label{eq:wide-band}
\end{aligned}$$ At most $k$ recorded occurrences of $w=A[t:H-K)$ have a full-text alignment identical to $A$.

